<div class="space-y-12">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-8">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight lg:text-5xl mb-4">
            <span class="text-transparent bg-clip-text bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-400 via-orange-400 to-amber-400 animate-gradient-x">
                SAML Single Sign-On
            </span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
            Sign users in through their organisation&rsquo;s identity provider &mdash; Okta, Microsoft Entra ID,
            Google Workspace, Keycloak, ADFS &mdash; over SAML 2.0.
        </p>
    </div>

    <section>
        <pre data-filename="Terminal"><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli generate auth    # SAML signs a User in
soli generate saml</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
            The application is the <strong class="text-white">service provider</strong> (SP): it sends the browser to the
            identity provider (IdP) with a request, and the IdP posts a signed response back. The cryptography and the
            protocol checks are the native <code class="text-amber-300">Saml</code> class; the generated files are the application&rsquo;s part,
            and yours to edit.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="generates" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">1</span>
            What it generates
        </h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">File</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/services/saml_sso.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SamlSso</code>: where the identity provider is, the request in flight, which account an assertion maps to</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/controllers/saml_controller.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>GET /saml/login</code>, <code>POST /saml/acs</code>, <code>GET /saml/metadata</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/models/saml_identity.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SamlIdentity</code>: the link between a <code>User</code> and <code>(issuer, NameID)</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>app/models/saml_assertion.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SamlAssertion</code>: the assertions that already signed someone in, kept until they expire</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>db/migrations/…_create_saml_identities.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Unique indexes on <code>(issuer, name_id)</code> and on <code>(user_id, issuer)</code> &mdash; one identity per account at each IdP</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>db/migrations/…_create_saml_assertions.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Index on <code>expires_at</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>config/routes.sl</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The three routes, and <code>skip_csrf("/saml/acs")</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Nothing existing is overwritten. Add the link to your sign-in view:</p>
        <pre data-filename="app/views/sessions/new.html.slv"><code class="language-erb text-sm">&lt;a href="/saml/login"&gt;Sign in with SSO&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="setup" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">2</span>
            Setup
        </h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><code>soli db:migrate up</code></li>
            <li>Set <code class="text-amber-300">APP_BASE_URL</code> to the <code>https://</code> origin the identity provider reaches the application at, and <code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_SESSION_SECRET</code> (32+ characters). <strong class="text-white">https is required</strong>, except on <code>localhost</code>: the request cookie must be <code>SameSite=None</code>, which a browser accepts only with <code>Secure</code>, and it keeps a <code>Secure</code> cookie over plain <code>http://</code> on <code>localhost</code> alone. On any other <code>http://</code> origin the cookie is dropped and every sign-in fails with &ldquo;no sign-in in progress&rdquo; (<code>/saml/login</code> logs a warning).</li>
            <li>Give the identity provider&rsquo;s administrator <code>https://your.app/saml/metadata</code>. It states the two values every IdP asks for: the <strong class="text-white">entity ID</strong> (audience), <code>APP_BASE_URL/saml/metadata</code> unless <code class="text-amber-300">SAML_SP_ENTITY_ID</code> overrides it, and the <strong class="text-white">ACS URL</strong> (reply URL), <code>APP_BASE_URL/saml/acs</code>. It asks for a <strong class="text-white">persistent NameID</strong> (<code>SAML_NAMEID_FORMAT</code> in <code>app/services/saml_sso.sl</code>): a sign-in is matched on <code>(issuer, NameID)</code>, and a persistent NameID is an opaque id the IdP never gives anyone else. With the <code>emailAddress</code> format the NameID <em>is</em> the mailbox, and the application inherits the IdP&rsquo;s address reuse: when the IdP hands <code>alice@corp.example</code> to a new employee, the new employee signs in to the old Alice&rsquo;s account. Configure the emailAddress format only if the IdP can send nothing else, and never reuses addresses.</li>
            <li>Point the application at the identity provider, either way:</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Variable</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it holds</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_IDP_METADATA_FILE</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Path to the IdP&rsquo;s metadata XML, inside the app (the server reads no file outside it)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_IDP_ENTITY_ID</code>, <code>SAML_IDP_SSO_URL</code>, <code>SAML_IDP_CERTIFICATE</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Or its three values: entity id, single sign-on URL (HTTP-Redirect binding), signing certificate (PEM; <code>\n</code> for newlines)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Without an entity id <code>SamlSso.identity_provider</code> raises, and no sign-in starts or finishes: it is what
            the <code>Issuer</code> of every response is checked against.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Then decide who gets an account:</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Variable</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it does</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_PROVISION</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>on</code>: a first sign-in creates the account; <code>off</code>: it does not. Unset, it follows <code>AUTH_SIGNUP</code> &mdash; <code>closed</code> provisions nobody, so the identity provider is no way around a sign-up you closed. Only <code>on</code> and <code>off</code> are read: any other value (<code>yes</code>, <code>true</code>) counts as <code>off</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_LINK_EXISTING</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>on</code>: a first sign-in may link the account that already has the asserted email. <strong class="text-white">Off unless set</strong></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_LINK_DIRECTORY</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>on</code>: a first sign-in may link, by email, an account a <a href="/docs/security/scim" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SCIM</a> directory provisioned. <strong class="text-white">Off unless set</strong>; off, such an account still links when its SCIM <code>externalId</code> is the assertion&rsquo;s NameID</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>SAML_SESSION_TTL</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">seconds a SAML sign-in lasts here, cut shorter by the IdP&rsquo;s <code>SessionNotOnOrAfter</code>. Default <code>28800</code> (8 hours)</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            In production with <code>AUTH_SIGNUP=first_user</code>, no SAML sign-in creates an account until the first one
            exists: the first account is the admin, made at <code>/signup</code> with <code>AUTH_SETUP_TOKEN</code>, and a SAML
            member created before it would make the app non-empty and close <code>/signup</code>. The refusal is logged.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The IdP must sign the assertion or the response (or both) with RSA-SHA256 &mdash; the metadata asks for signed
            assertions (<code>WantAssertionsSigned="true"</code>), and a response signed as a whole is accepted too. It must
            send an email address: in an attribute named <code>email</code>, <code>mail</code>, <code>emailaddress</code>, or
            the usual claim URIs (the first of <code>SAML_EMAIL_ATTRIBUTES</code>, in that order, that the assertion carries
            with a value), or as the NameID when its format is
            <code>urn:oasis:names:tc:SAML:1.1:nameid-format:emailAddress</code> (a NameID in any other format &mdash; a
            username, an opaque id &mdash; is never taken for an address, even with an <code>@</code> in it). An address with
            anything but ASCII in it is refused: lowercasing folds some Unicode letters into ASCII ones (the Kelvin sign
            <code>&#8490;</code> into <code>k</code>), and two different addresses would reach the same account.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            An <code>emailAddress</code>-format NameID is stored and looked up trimmed and lowercased, so an IdP that starts
            sending <code>Alice@corp.example</code> for the identity stored as <code>alice@corp.example</code> still signs her in.
            An identity stored exactly by an older version of the scaffold is found too (the lookup tries the normalised
            NameID, then the exact one). A NameID in any other format is opaque and matched exactly.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="flow" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">3</span>
            How a sign-in runs
        </h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><code>/saml/login</code> builds the request (<code class="text-amber-300">Saml.auth_request</code>) and redirects to the IdP. The request id and the <code>return_to</code> path go in an encrypted cookie.</li>
            <li>The user authenticates at the IdP, which posts <code>SAMLResponse</code> to <code>/saml/acs</code>.</li>
            <li><code>SamlSso.finish</code> reads the request id from the cookie and <code class="text-amber-300">Saml.verify_response</code> checks the response against it. Only once it verifies is the cookie cleared &mdash; a request is answered once &mdash; so a forged post to <code>/saml/acs</code>, which any site can make a browser send, does not cancel the sign-in in progress. <code>SamlSso.spend</code> then records the assertion&rsquo;s id until it expires: a response posted again, even with a copy of the request cookie, is refused.</li>
            <li><code>SamlSso.user_for</code> finds the account: the one already linked to this <code>(issuer, NameID)</code>; else the one with that email, linked from now on, when <code class="text-amber-300">SAML_LINK_EXISTING=on</code>, when a directory provisioned it and named this identity (or <code>SAML_LINK_DIRECTORY=on</code>), or when nobody confirmed its address (see below); else a new one (see <code>SAML_PROVISION</code>). Otherwise an address that already has an account is refused: link it by hand (<code>SamlIdentity.create({"user_id", "issuer", "name_id", "email"})</code>). The link must be stored, or the sign-in is refused (and an account just created for it is deleted). The password lockout does not apply here: it counts wrong passwords, and the IdP authenticated the user.
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2">
                    <li><strong class="text-white">A new account</strong> is a <code>member</code>, confirmed, with no password, and <code>sso_only: true</code>: it signs in through the IdP only (see <a href="#sso-only" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">what <code>sso_only</code> blocks</a>).</li>
                    <li><strong class="text-white">Linking by email</strong> (<code>SAML_LINK_EXISTING=on</code>) trusts the IdP with every address it asserts. Turn it on only when its users cannot edit that attribute. Even then, an admin&rsquo;s account is never linked by its email, nor an account already linked to another identity at the same IdP: the address now names someone else there (a recycled mailbox), not the account&rsquo;s owner. A linked confirmed account stays as it was &mdash; its password, if it has one, still works, and it is not marked <code>sso_only</code>.</li>
                    <li><strong class="text-white">A directory account</strong> &mdash; one <a href="/docs/security/scim" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SCIM</a> created (<code>scim_provisioned_at</code>) or gave an <code>externalId</code> &mdash; is linked by its first SAML sign-in with <code>SAML_LINK_EXISTING</code> off when its <code>externalId</code> is the assertion&rsquo;s NameID: the directory named this very identity. By email alone it is linked only with <code>SAML_LINK_DIRECTORY=on</code>, which says the directory and the IdP are one organisation&rsquo;s and its users cannot edit their email attribute &mdash; otherwise an IdP user who sets someone&rsquo;s address would take that account. With no password at that point it becomes <code>sso_only</code>. An admin&rsquo;s is still refused.</li>
                    <li><strong class="text-white">An unconfirmed account</strong> is linked with linking off only where it cannot have been used: when <code>auth_require_confirmed_email</code> is <code>true</code> (nobody signs in to an unconfirmed account, so it is a placeholder someone typed) and a first sign-in may create accounts (<code>SAML_PROVISION</code>). It then loses every credential (<code>SamlSso.take_over_unconfirmed</code> calls <code>User#revoke_credentials</code>, which the OAuth scaffold uses too) and becomes <code>sso_only</code>. With the default &mdash; unconfirmed accounts sign in &mdash; most real accounts are &ldquo;unconfirmed&rdquo;, and handing one to whoever the identity provider says has that address is the takeover linking-off exists to prevent: the sign-in is refused, and the log says to confirm or delete the account, or to require confirmation. <strong class="text-white">Turn <code>auth_require_confirmed_email</code> on with SAML</strong> if sign-up is open, so that an address registered in wait does not block its owner. An admin&rsquo;s account is refused either way.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>A new session is opened and the browser goes to <code>return_to</code> (a path on this site, or <code>/</code>).</li>
        </ol>
        <h3 id="revoke-credentials" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">What <code>revoke_credentials</code> removes</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code>User#revoke_credentials</code> deletes, where the app has the model, every row tied to the account &mdash;
            <code>OauthIdentity</code>, <code>SamlIdentity</code>, <code>PasskeyCredential</code>, <code>ApiToken</code>,
            <code>TwoFactor</code>, <code>MagicLink</code>, <code>Device</code> (push registrations), by <code>user_id</code>; the
            OpenID Connect provider&rsquo;s <code>OauthConsent</code>, <code>OauthAuthorizationCode</code>,
            <code>OauthDeviceCode</code>, <code>OauthRefreshToken</code>, by <code>user_key</code> &mdash; then clears the
            password, a pending reset link and the remember-me digest, and bumps <code>session_version</code>, which ends every
            open session. The list is <code>USER_CREDENTIAL_MODELS</code> in <code>app/models/user.sl</code>, as
            <code>[class, key field]</code> pairs: add your own models there. The rows go first and the account last, and a
            failure raises: a takeover that stops halfway leaves the account as it was and links nothing, and running it again
            finishes it.
        </p>
        <h3 id="sso-only" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">What <code>sso_only</code> blocks</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            An <code>sso_only</code> account signs in through the identity provider only. It gets no password sign-in (refused
            with the same answer and the same Argon2id cost as a wrong password, and wrong passwords typed for it are not
            counted, so nobody can lock it), no password reset, no magic link (asking answers as for anyone, and a link issued
            before is refused), no new API token (<code>403</code>) and no use of one made before, no passkey registration
            (<code>403</code>) and no sign-in with one registered before.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            It does <strong class="text-white">not</strong> end what is already open at once. A session opened through the IdP lasts
            until its end after the user is removed there &mdash; <code>SAML_SESSION_TTL</code> (8 hours by default) or the IdP&rsquo;s
            <code>SessionNotOnOrAfter</code>, whichever is sooner; the ACS stores it as <code>sign_in_expires_at</code>, which
            <code>load_current_user</code> enforces &mdash; and an OAuth identity already linked to the account still signs in.
            Ending those at once takes a <a href="/docs/security/scim" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SCIM</a> deactivation
            (every request of a deactivated user is a guest&rsquo;s) or <code>revoke_credentials</code>. An app whose auth scaffold
            predates <code>sign_in_expires_at</code> needs the check copied into <code>app/middleware/current_user.sl</code>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A refused response renders the sign-in page with a generic message and a 401; the reason goes to the log
            (<code>SAML sign-in refused: …</code>).
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Why a cookie and not the session.</strong> The response is a cross-site POST, and a
            <code>SameSite=Lax</code> session cookie does not accompany one. The request cookie is
            <code>SameSite=None; Secure; HttpOnly; Path=/</code>, encrypted, and lives ten minutes. With an <code>https://</code>
            <code>APP_BASE_URL</code> it is named <code>__Host-saml_request</code>: a browser then keeps it only Secure, for this
            host alone, with no <code>Domain</code>, so a sibling subdomain cannot plant or overwrite it. Over plain
            <code>http://</code> (local development on <code>localhost</code>) a browser drops a <code>__Host-</code> cookie, so
            there it is plain <code>saml_request</code> &mdash; still <code>Secure</code>, which <code>localhost</code> accepts and
            every other plain-http origin does not. For
            the same reason <code>/saml/acs</code> is exempt from the CSRF origin gate: what authenticates that request is the
            signature.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">With two-factor.</strong> A user who turned on
            <a href="/docs/security/authentication#two-factor" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">two-factor</a> is asked for their code after a SAML sign-in
            too. If the identity provider already enforces it, mark the session in <code>SamlController#acs</code>:
            <code>session_set("two_factor", "ok:#{user._key}")</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="verified" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">4</span>
            What is verified
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-300">Saml.verify_response</code> raises unless all of this holds:</p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Signature.</strong> The assertion, the response, or both are signed, and every signature present verifies with a certificate you configured &mdash; never with the one the message carries. A certificate with an RSA key under 2048 bits is refused.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">No signature wrapping.</strong> A signature is only looked for as a direct child of the response or of its one assertion, it must reference that parent&rsquo;s <code>ID</code>, and the claims are read from the very node the digest was computed over. A second assertion, a duplicated <code>ID</code>, or a signature that points elsewhere is refused.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">For this application.</strong> <code>Audience</code> is your entity id; <code>Recipient</code> and <code>Destination</code> are your ACS URL; <code>Issuer</code> is the IdP you name in <code>idp_entity_id</code>. That key is required: an empty string raises (an unset environment variable is a missing setting, not &ldquo;any IdP&rdquo;), and accepting any issuer the certificate signs for takes <code>any_issuer: true</code>, said by name.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">For this request.</strong> <code>InResponseTo</code> is the id of the request this browser started. A response nobody asked for (IdP-initiated) is refused unless you pass <code>allow_unsolicited: true</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Now.</strong> <code>NotBefore</code> / <code>NotOnOrAfter</code> on the conditions and on the subject confirmation, with 120 seconds of clock skew (<code>clock_skew</code>, up to 600).</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Successful.</strong> The status is <code>Success</code>; otherwise the error names the status.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Reasonable in shape.</strong> Before any of the above is walked, a response with more than 10,000 elements, more than 64 distinct namespace prefixes or more than 30,000 namespace declarations is refused. A real one has a few dozen elements and half a dozen prefixes (an IdP that repeats <code>xmlns:xs</code> and <code>xmlns:xsi</code> on every <code>AttributeValue</code> makes two declarations per value, and passes); through 2.39.2 a 1&nbsp;MiB unsigned response with thousands of prefixes cost tens of seconds of CPU. A signature&rsquo;s <code>SignedInfo</code> must hold its <code>CanonicalizationMethod</code>, its <code>SignatureMethod</code> and one <code>Reference</code>, nothing else, and an <code>InclusiveNamespaces</code> <code>PrefixList</code> may name at most 64 prefixes: before that limit, an unsigned response whose transform listed a hundred thousand prefixes cost minutes of CPU before the key was consulted.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Comments inside a value do not split it: <code>user@example.com&lt;!----&gt;.evil.com</code> reads as the whole
            signed text. A namespace prefix bound to an empty URI (<code>xmlns:saml=""</code>, which XML 1.0 forbids) or used
            without a declaration is refused: the canonical form cannot express either, so through 2.38.0 such a declaration
            could hide a signed element from the reader without changing the digest.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            <code class="text-amber-300">verify_response</code> checks one response; it keeps no state. That an assertion signs
            in only once is the application&rsquo;s to enforce &mdash; <code>soli generate saml</code> does it with
            <code>SamlSso.spend</code>, keyed on <code>assertion_id</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="class" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">5</span>
            The Saml class
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Stateless, like <a href="/docs/builtins/webauthn" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300"><code>WebAuthn</code></a>. Every failed check raises.
        </p>

        <h3 id="auth-request" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Saml.auth_request(options)</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Soli"><code class="language-soli text-sm">request = Saml.auth_request({
  "idp_sso_url": "https://idp.example.com/sso",
  "sp_entity_id": "https://app.example.com/saml/metadata",
  "acs_url": "https://app.example.com/saml/acs"
})
request["id"]    # "_3f1c…" — keep it; the response must answer it
request["url"]   # redirect the browser here (HTTP-Redirect binding)
request["xml"]   # the &lt;samlp:AuthnRequest&gt;</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">Options: <code>relay_state</code> (up to 80 bytes, returned by the IdP as <code>RelayState</code>), <code>force_authn: true</code>, <code>name_id_format</code>.</p>

        <h3 id="verify-response" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Saml.verify_response(saml_response, expected)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4"><code>saml_response</code> is the <code>SAMLResponse</code> form field as posted (base64).</p>
        <pre data-filename="Soli"><code class="language-soli text-sm">assertion = Saml.verify_response(params["SAMLResponse"], {
  "certificates": [idp_certificate],          # PEM or bare base64; or "certificate"
  "idp_entity_id": "https://idp.example.com/metadata",
  "audience": "https://app.example.com/saml/metadata",
  "acs_url": "https://app.example.com/saml/acs",
  "request_id": request_id
})</code></pre>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 mb-4">
            <table class="w-full text-sm text-left">
                <thead class="bg-white/5 text-gray-300">
                    <tr><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">Key</th><th class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold">What it holds</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/10 text-gray-400">
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>name_id</code>, <code>name_id_format</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The subject</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>name_qualifier</code>, <code>sp_name_qualifier</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The NameID&rsquo;s <code>NameQualifier</code> / <code>SPNameQualifier</code> (<code>nil</code> when absent): what scopes a persistent or transient id &mdash; the same value under another qualifier is another principal</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>attributes</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>{name: [values]}</code> &mdash; always arrays</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>issuer</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The identity provider&rsquo;s entity id</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>session_index</code>, <code>session_expires_at</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The IdP session (<code>nil</code> when not stated; times are Unix seconds)</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>authn_context</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The <code>AuthnContextClassRef</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>assertion_id</code>, <code>in_response_to</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Ids</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>response_id</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The response&rsquo;s id &mdash; <code>nil</code> when only the assertion is signed, since nothing vouches for it then</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>expires_at</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">When the assertion stops being acceptable: the earlier of the conditions&rsquo; and the bearer confirmation&rsquo;s <code>NotOnOrAfter</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>signed</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>"assertion"</code>, <code>"response"</code> or <code>"both"</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>certificate_expires_at</code>, <code>certificate_expired</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">The <code>notAfter</code> (Unix seconds) of the certificate whose key signed the claims, and whether it has passed &mdash; watch it and install the IdP&rsquo;s next certificate before it lapses</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            Other <code>expected</code> keys: <code>allow_unsolicited</code>, <code>any_issuer</code> (skip the
            <code>Issuer</code> check when <code>idp_entity_id</code> is not given), <code>clock_skew</code>,
            <code>allow_sha1</code> (SHA-1 signatures are refused otherwise), <code>require_valid_certificate</code> (refuse a signature made with a certificate outside its validity period &mdash; off by default, because SAML treats the certificate as a key container and identity providers do sign with expired ones; the result reports it either way), <code>now</code> (Unix seconds, for specs).
            Listing several certificates accepts any of them &mdash; that is how an IdP&rsquo;s certificate is rotated without
            downtime.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            The certificate is a pinned key, not a chain: its validity dates are not checked, so an expired certificate you
            still list keeps verifying. Remove a certificate from the list to stop trusting it.
        </p>

        <h3 id="metadata" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Saml.metadata(options)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">The service provider&rsquo;s metadata XML, from <code>sp_entity_id</code> and <code>acs_url</code>. <code>name_id_format</code> defaults to <code>emailAddress</code>; the generated <code>SamlSso.metadata</code> passes <code>persistent</code> (see <a href="#setup" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">Setup</a>).</p>

        <h3 id="parse-metadata" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Saml.parse_metadata(xml)</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Soli"><code class="language-soli text-sm">idp = Saml.parse_metadata(File.read("idp-metadata.xml"))
idp["entity_id"]      # "https://idp.example.com/metadata"
idp["sso_url"]        # the HTTP-Redirect SingleSignOnService
idp["certificates"]   # signing certificates, PEM</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">The metadata&rsquo;s own signature is not checked: read it from a file you were given or an <code>https://</code> URL you trust.</p>

        <h3 id="test-response" class="text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Saml.test_response(options)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
            A signed response, as an identity provider would post it &mdash; the stand-in a spec signs in with. It signs what
            it is told with a key you supply
            (<code>openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout idp.key -out idp.crt -days 3650 -subj "/CN=test"</code>).
        </p>
        <pre data-filename="Soli"><code class="language-soli text-sm">response = Saml.test_response({
  "private_key": File.read("tests/fixtures/idp.key"),
  "issuer": "https://idp.test/metadata",
  "acs_url": "https://app.test/saml/acs",
  "audience": "https://app.test/saml/metadata",
  "name_id": "ada@example.com",
  "request_id": request_id,
  "attributes": {"groups": ["admin", "staff"]}
})</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-400 my-4">Options: <code>sign</code> (<code>"assertion"</code> by default, <code>"response"</code>, <code>"both"</code>, <code>"none"</code>), <code>name_id_format</code>, <code>session_index</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>now</code>, <code>lifetime</code> (seconds, 300), <code>response_id</code>, <code>assertion_id</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2 id="limits" class="flex items-center gap-3 text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-20">
            <span class="flex items-center justify-center w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-amber-500/20 text-amber-400 text-sm border border-amber-500/30">6</span>
            Limits
        </h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-400">
            <li><strong class="text-white">One identity provider per application.</strong> For one per customer, make <code>SamlSso.identity_provider</code> read it from the tenant instead of the environment &mdash; and scope the accounts per tenant too (a database per tenant, or a tenant key on <code>User</code> and <code>SamlIdentity</code> that <code>user_for</code> filters on). Keep <code>SAML_LINK_EXISTING</code> off there: each customer&rsquo;s IdP asserts whatever addresses its own administrator types, so linking by email would let one tenant&rsquo;s IdP sign in to another tenant&rsquo;s accounts.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Not supported, and refused by name:</strong> encrypted assertions and <code>EncryptedID</code> (turn assertion encryption off at the IdP), ECDSA and RSA-PSS signatures, SHA-384, inclusive canonicalization. <code>SignatureMethod</code> takes only <code>rsa-sha256</code>/<code>rsa-sha512</code> (and <code>rsa-sha1</code> with <code>allow_sha1</code>), <code>DigestMethod</code> only <code>sha256</code>/<code>sha512</code> (and <code>sha1</code>, also only with <code>allow_sha1</code>).</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Requests are not signed</strong>, and the HTTP-POST binding is not offered for them. Most identity providers accept unsigned requests.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">No single logout.</strong> Signing out of the application does not sign out of the identity provider, and the reverse. A SAML session here ends after <code>SAML_SESSION_TTL</code> instead.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">No account deprovisioning.</strong> A user removed at the IdP can no longer sign in through it, and an <code>sso_only</code> account has no other way in; but the account and its open sessions remain &mdash; that is what <a href="/docs/security/scim" class="text-amber-400 hover:text-amber-300">SCIM</a> is for.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Attributes are not mapped to roles.</strong> <code>assertion["attributes"]</code> is there in <code>SamlSso.user_for</code> if you want to.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>
